#open-question(title: [Does Within-Patient Recovery Ratio Track PEM Episodes?])[
The monitoring claim rests on an inference, not on evidence. All three cohorts compared groups at one time point. None measured the same patient repeatedly across weeks, and none recorded whether a low recovery ratio preceded, coincided with, or followed a PEM episode.

The proposed test is simple and cheap: home or clinic dynamometry once or twice weekly for 8–12 weeks, with daily symptom and PEM diaries, in 30–50 patients. The analysis asks whether a fall in recovery ratio precedes a self-reported crash by 12–72 hours — the temporal pattern that PEM requires. A cross-sectional correlation with symptom severity would not answer the question, because it cannot show direction.

Two design traps must be avoided. First, the act of testing is itself an exertion; a protocol that asks for maximal grips twice weekly may create the crashes it is meant to predict. A submaximal variant may be necessary (see @oq:handgrip-severe-submax-protocol). Second, a ratio that tracks *general* symptom burden is not a PEM monitor; the analysis must separate baseline severity from acute change.

*Prediction:* a drop in recovery ratio precedes self-reported PEM onset by 12–72 hours in a majority of events, with a larger effect than changes in absolute force. *Falsified if:* recovery ratio changes only concurrently with symptoms, or does not change around PEM events at all.

*Certainty: 0.20.* (The monitoring use is extrapolated from cross-sectional group differences; no prospective data exist.)

*Consequence:* Until this test is run, a patient's handgrip number describes today, not tomorrow — clinicians should not present it as an early-warning system.
] <oq:handgrip-prospective-pem-tracking>
